er of Historic Places as Alaska Totems on June 21, 1971.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/THCTotems.JPG/960px-THCTotems.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Totem Heritage Center"},{"n":"Saxman Totem Park","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":55.318965,"lo":-131.598144,"d":3822,"wd":"Q18331580","ad":"2706 South Tongass Highway","ds":"Saxman Totem Park is a public park in the city of Saxman, Alaska, just south of Ketchikan in southeastern Alaska. The park is home to a collection of totem poles, some of which are old poles relocated to this place from unoccupied Tlingit villages in the region, or were reconstructed by skilled Tlingit carvers under the auspices of the Civilian Conservation Corps in the 1930s.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Saxman_totem_park%28js%2902.jpg/960px-Saxman_totem_park%28js%2902.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Saxman Totem Park"},{"n":"Wolf Point","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":55.362211,"lo":-131.708867,"d":4731},{"n":"Ketchikan Dry Goods","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":55.340946,"lo":-131.640548,"d":194},{"n":"First Bank Main Branch","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":55.342253,"lo":-131.646897,"d":248,"ad":"331 Dock Street"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":55.341067,"lo":-131.647016,"d":274},{"n":"Ketchikan Tech center","g":"essentials","k":"college","la":55.339067,"lo":-131.637755,"d":465,"wifi":1},{"n":"Ketchikan Public Library","g":"essentials","k":"library","la":55.349175,"lo":-131.651059,"d":946,"h":"Mo-We 10:00-20:00, Th-Sa 10:00-18:00; Su 13:00-17:00","w":"https://www.ketchikanpubliclibrary.org/","ph":"+1-907-225-3331","ad":"1110 Copper Ridge Lane","wifi":1},{"n":"My Office Sports Bar","g":"essentials","k":"nightclub","la":55.348536,"lo":-131.671079,"d":1918,"w":"https://www.facebook.com/myofficesportsbar","ad":"2050 Sea Level Drive","wifi":1},{"n":"First Bank Financial Center","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":55.349197,"lo":-131.670685,"d":1924,"ad":"2030 Sea Level Drive, Suite 300"},{"n":"AT&T","g":"essentials","k":"mobile_phone","la":55.348762,"lo":-131.672176,"d":1992},{"n":"GCI","g":"essentials","k":"mobile_phone","la":55.348969,"lo":-131.672878,"d":2041},{"n":"Safeway","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":55.348417,"lo":-131.673261,"d":2042,"w":"https://local.safeway.com/safeway/ak/ketchikan/2417-tongass-ave.html","ph":"+1 907-228-1900","ad":"2417 Tongass Avenue"},{"n":"First Bank Totem Branch","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":55.350137,"lo":-131.675974,"d":2272,"ad":"2530 Tongass Avenue"},{"n":"Ketchikan Creek","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":55.3408,"lo":-131.6397,"d":247,"ds":"Ketchikan Creek (alternate, \"Fish Creek\"; Tlingit, \"Kitschkhin\") is a salmon spawning stream on Revillagigedo Island in the U.S. state of Alaska. It heads in a lake and travels through downtown Ketchikan 6 miles (9.7 km) to Tongass Narrows. The historic Creek Street in Ketchikan runs along the creek banks as a piling-perched boardwalk.","wpu":"Ketchikan Creek","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Ketichikan-Creek-Street.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"First Lutheran Church (Ketchikan, Alaska)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":55.345278,"lo":-131.657778,"d":1002,"ds":"The First Lutheran Church in Ketchikan, Alaska, is a historic church at 1200 Tongass Avenue. It was designed by architect W.G. Brust of Seattle and was built in 1930 by Ketchikan local builder Carl Foss. It is a two-story wood-frame structure, with a three-story tower at its southwest corner.","wpu":"First Lutheran Church (Ketchikan, Alaska)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/First_lutheran_church_ketchikan.jpg/960px-First_lutheran_church_ketchikan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Gravina Island Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":55.3516,"lo":-131.7158,"d":4725,"ds":"The Gravina Island Bridge, commonly referred to as the \"Bridge to Nowhere\", was a proposed bridge to replace the ferry that currently connects the town of Ketchikan, Alaska, United States, with Gravina Island, an island that contains the Ketchikan International Airport as well as 50 residents. The bridge was projected to cost $398 million.","wpu":"Gravina Island Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Sarah_palin_signature.svg/960px-Sarah_palin_signature.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Creek Street is a historic area of Ketchikan, Alaska. The street is actually a boardwalk mounted in stilts on a high slope on the east side of Ketchikan Creek, east of the city's downtown. Creek Street was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2014.","wpu":"Creek Street (Ketchikan, Alaska)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Creek_Street%28js%2906.jpg/960px-Creek_Street%28js%2906.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}